    id: 'seed-blue-ridge-parkway-highlights',
    slug: 'blue-ridge-parkway-highlights',
    title: 'Blue Ridge Parkway: Mile-by-Mile Highlights',
    excerpt: 'The essential stops along the 469-mile Blue Ridge Parkway, from Shenandoah to the Smokies: the overlooks, hikes, and landmarks worth your time.',
    paragraphs: [
    'The Blue Ridge Parkway is 469 miles of slow road connecting Shenandoah National Park in Virginia to Great Smoky Mountains National Park in North Carolina. No billboards, no commercial traffic, a 45 mph speed limit — it was designed in the 1930s as a scenic drive and it still works exactly as intended. Driving the whole thing takes three to five days if you actually stop; most people do a section. Here are the highlights, in milepost order from north to south.',
    'Virginia: Mileposts 0–216',
    'MP 0 — Rockfish Gap. The northern terminus, where the Parkway meets Skyline Drive and I-64 near Waynesboro. If you are starting here, fill up on gas and food — services on the Parkway itself are essentially nonexistent.',
    'MP 6 — Humpback Rocks. A short, steep 2-mile round-trip hike to a rocky summit with big views back over the Shenandoah Valley. The adjacent 19th-century farmstead, with log cabins and a blacksmith shop, is worth 30 minutes.',
    'MP 86 — Peaks of Otter. One of the Parkway\'s classic stops: a lodge, a lake, and three peaks. The Sharp Top hike (3.3 miles round trip, steep) ends at a 360-degree summit. The lake trail is flat and easy. Elk and deer are common at dawn and dusk.',
    'MP 120 — Roanoke area. The Parkway passes near Roanoke, the largest city directly on the route — useful for gas, groceries, and lodging. The Roanoke River overlook and the Mill Mountain Star viewpoint (a short detour) are worth it.',
    'MP 176 — Mabry Mill. The most photographed spot on the entire Parkway: a gristmill and sawmill from the early 1900s, with a blacksmith shop and demonstrations in season. Go early or late for the light and smaller crowds. The adjacent restaurant serves country cooking.',
    'MP 189–216 — Meadows of Dan to the state line. Rolling farmland, split-rail fences, and some of the Parkway\'s prettiest pastoral scenery. Groundhog Mountain (MP 188) has an old tobacco barn and observation tower.',
    'North Carolina, part 1: Mileposts 216–305',
    'MP 240s — Doughton Park. One of the Parkway\'s best-kept secrets: a large high-country park with meadows, the historic Brinegar Cabin, and excellent hiking. The Bluff Mountain Trail and the trail to the cabin are both worthwhile. Far fewer crowds than the marquee stops.',
    'MP 294 — Moses H. Cone Memorial Park. A 3,500-acre estate with a manor house (now a craft shop run by the Southern Highland Craft Guild), 25 miles of carriage trails, and Trout Lake. The manor\'s wraparound porch is one of the great sitting spots on the Parkway. The carriage roads are perfect for easy walking and, in fall, spectacular.',
    'MP 304 — Linn Cove Viaduct. The engineering marvel of the Parkway: a 1,243-foot S-curved bridge that wraps around the side of Grandfather Mountain, built segment by segment to avoid scarring the slope. The views from the bridge itself are stunning; the best photos of it are from the Linn Cove visitor center trail below.',
    'MP 305 — Grandfather Mountain. Just off the Parkway via US 221, this is a private nature park with the famous Mile High Swinging Bridge, wildlife habitats (black bears, cougars, eagles), and serious hiking — the Grandfather Trail is one of the most challenging and rewarding hikes in the East, with ladders and cables across the ridgeline. Admission is charged; allow half a day.',
    'MP 316 — Linville Falls. The most popular waterfall hike on the Parkway, and deservedly so. Multiple overlooks (Erwin\'s View, Chimney View) via easy-to-moderate trails show the falls plunging into the Linville Gorge — the "Grand Canyon of the East." The full loop to all overlooks is about 2 miles.',
    'North Carolina, part 2: Mileposts 305–469',
    'MP 355 — Mount Mitchell. A 5-mile spur road (NC 128) climbs to the highest peak east of the Mississippi (6,684 feet). The observation deck gives 360-degree views; on clear days you can see 85 miles. The spruce-fir forest at the top feels more Canadian than Carolinian. Even in summer, bring a jacket — it can be 20 degrees cooler than Asheville.',
    'MP 364 — Craggy Gardens. Rhododendron tunnels, twisted beech trees, and a bald summit with long views. In mid-to-late June the rhododendrons bloom in a spectacle that rivals fall color. The short Craggy Pinnacle trail (1.4 miles round trip) is the payoff hike.',
    'MP 382 — Folk Art Center. The headquarters of the Southern Highland Craft Guild, with galleries, demonstrations, and a shop full of work by Appalachian craftspeople. A good rainy-day or leg-stretch stop.',
    'MP 384 — Asheville. The Parkway\'s urban interlude. Asheville itself deserves a day: the River Arts District, downtown\'s Art Deco architecture, and the Biltmore Estate (America\'s largest private home, just off the Parkway corridor). This is also the best resupply point on the southern half.',
    'MP 418 — Graveyard Fields. A high-elevation valley with waterfalls (Second Falls is a short hike from the parking area), wild blueberries in late summer, and boardwalk trails through mountain bogs. One of the most distinctive landscapes on the Parkway.',
    'MP 451 — Waterrock Knob. The last big viewpoint before the terminus: a 6,292-foot summit with a short, steep trail to 360-degree views. A favorite sunset spot.',
    'MP 469 — Oconaluftee. The southern terminus, at the entrance to Great Smoky Mountains National Park. The Mountain Farm Museum (a collection of historic log buildings) and the Oconaluftee Visitor Center are here, and elk are frequently visible in the fields at dawn and dusk — reintroduced to the Smokies starting in 2001, they are now a reliable sight.',
    'Driving it well',
    'Speed and time. The 45 mph limit is strictly enforced and the road is winding; average 30–35 mph in practice. Budget at least three days for the full drive with stops, five if you hike.',
    'When to go. October is peak — spectacular color, peak crowds, book lodging far ahead. Late April through May brings wildflowers and waterfalls at full flow with a fraction of the traffic. Summer is pleasant at elevation (the high sections stay cool) but hazy. Winter: sections close due to ice and snow, especially the high elevations around Craggy Gardens and Waterrock Knob; check the Parkway\'s road-closure map before going.',
    'Fuel and food. There are no gas stations on the Parkway. Fill up in Waynesboro, Roanoke, Boone/Blowing Rock, or Asheville before committing to long stretches. Food is similarly scarce — the lodges at Peaks of Otter and the restaurants at Mabry Mill and elsewhere are seasonal. Pack a cooler; the overlooks are made for picnics.',
    'Lodging. The Parkway has a handful of lodges (Peaks of Otter, Bluffs Lodge at Doughton Park), all seasonal and booked early. Otherwise, base in the gateway towns: Waynesboro, Roanoke, Boone/Blowing Rock, Asheville, or Cherokee at the southern end.',
    'Hiking. The Mountains-to-Sea Trail parallels much of the Parkway and offers endless options. For short walks, almost every visitor center has a trailhead nearby. Carry water — sources on the ridge are few.',
    'The Blue Ridge Parkway is not a road you drive to get somewhere. It is the destination, taken at 35 miles an hour, with the windows down. Plan for slow, stop often, and let the mileposts be suggestions rather than a checklist.',
    ],
