    id: 'seed-great-lakes-which-one-first',
    slug: 'great-lakes-which-one-first',
    title: 'The Great Lakes: Which One to Visit First',
    excerpt: 'Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie, Ontario — an honest comparison of the five Great Lakes to help you choose your first trip.',
    paragraphs: [
    'The five Great Lakes hold about a fifth of the world\'s surface fresh water. Together they are an inland sea — you cannot see across most of them, they generate their own weather, and their shorelines span eight states and two countries. But the lakes are profoundly different from each other: different water, different landscapes, different trips. If you are planning a first Great Lakes visit, here is how to choose.',
    'Lake Superior: the wilderness lake',
    'Superior is the largest, deepest, and coldest of the lakes — and the wildest. Its water is famously clear and famously frigid; surface temperatures rarely break 60°F even in August, and the lake has claimed enough ships to fill museums (the Edmund Fitzgerald went down in 1975 in a November gale, and Gordon Lightfoot\'s song keeps the memory alive).',
    'What it offers: The most dramatic scenery of any lake. Pictured Rocks National Lakeshore in Michigan\'s Upper Peninsula — 15 miles of sandstone cliffs stained red, orange, and green by mineral seep, best seen by boat tour from Munising. The Apostle Islands off Wisconsin\'s Bayfield Peninsula, with sea caves carved into red sandstone. Minnesota\'s North Shore, from Duluth to the Canadian border: Split Rock Lighthouse on its cliff, Gooseberry Falls, Temperance River gorge, and the Superior Hiking Trail. Isle Royale National Park — a roadless island wilderness in the middle of the lake, reachable only by boat or seaplane.',
    'Choose Superior if: you want wilderness, hiking, waterfalls, and the feeling of the edge of the map. Know that: the water is too cold for casual swimming, the weather turns fast, and the Upper Peninsula and North Shore are remote — plan fuel and lodging.',
    'Lake Michigan: the beach lake',
    'The only Great Lake entirely within the United States, and the one with the best beaches. Its eastern shore — Michigan\'s "Gold Coast" — has freshwater beaches that genuinely rival ocean coasts: wide sand, dunes, sunsets over open water.',
    'What it offers: Sleeping Bear Dunes National Lakeshore — towering sand dunes above impossibly blue water, named "Most Beautiful Place in America" in a 2011 viewer poll, with the famous Dune Climb and the Pierce Stocking Scenic Drive. Traverse City, the cherry capital, with wineries on the Leelanau and Old Mission peninsulas. Mackinac Island at the straits — no cars allowed, reached by ferry, all Victorian hotels and fudge shops and bicycles. On the Wisconsin side: Door County, the "Cape Cod of the Midwest," with its fish boils, cherry orchards, and 300 miles of shoreline. Chicago\'s lakefront — 18 miles of beaches, parks, and the Lakefront Trail — is the urban bookend.',
    'Choose Michigan if: you want the classic summer lake vacation — beaches, dunes, small resort towns, wineries. Know that: summer weekends in Traverse City, Door County, and the popular dune areas are crowded and pricey; book lodging early.',
    'Lake Huron: the quiet lake',
    'Huron is the second-largest by area and the least visited by tourists — which is either a drawback or the entire point. Its defining feature is its shoreline complexity: some 30,000 islands, including Manitoulin Island (the world\'s largest freshwater island, on the Canadian side) and the Les Cheneaux Islands on Michigan\'s Upper Peninsula.',
    'What it offers: Georgian Bay\'s Thirty Thousand Islands (Canadian side) — a boater\'s labyrinth of granite and pine. On the U.S. side: the Thunder Bay National Marine Sanctuary off Alpena, protecting nearly 100 known shipwrecks in clear shallow water — excellent for diving, snorkeling, and glass-bottom boat tours. Mackinaw City and the Straits of Mackinac (shared with Lake Michigan). The Sunrise Side of Michigan\'s Lower Peninsula — Tawas, Oscoda, Alpena — is affordable, uncrowded, and genuinely pleasant, with long shallow beaches good for families.',
    'Choose Huron if: you want quiet water, boating, shipwrecks, and small towns without the crowds or prices of Lake Michigan\'s resort coast. Know that: the tourist infrastructure is thinner — fewer restaurants, fewer lodging options, longer drives between towns.',
    'Lake Erie: the warm lake',
    'The shallowest Great Lake (average depth 62 feet), which makes it the warmest — the only lake where swimming is reliably comfortable all summer. It is also the most urbanized, ringed by Cleveland, Toledo, Detroit\'s suburbs, and Buffalo.',
    'What it offers: The Lake Erie Islands — Put-in-Bay on South Bass Island (lively, bar-heavy, the Perry\'s Victory and International Peace Memorial commemorating the War of 1812 naval battle), quieter Middle Bass and Kelleys Island (glacial grooves, wineries, state park). The walleye fishing is famously among the best in North America — charter boats run from Port Clinton and the western basin. Presque Isle State Park in Erie, Pennsylvania: a recurving sand spit with beaches, lagoons, and 13 miles of trails. Point Pelee, Ontario (Canadian side): the southernmost point of mainland Canada, a major migratory-bird hotspot.',
    'Choose Erie if: you want warm water, fishing, island-hopping, and easy access from major cities. Know that: the shoreline is more developed and less scenic than the northern lakes; algae blooms have affected the western basin in some summers — check conditions.',
    'Lake Ontario: the city lake',
    'The smallest by surface area but deep, and the most international — Toronto\'s skyline rises directly from its northwestern shore, and the lake connects to the Atlantic via the St. Lawrence Seaway.',
    'What it offers: Niagara Falls sits between Erie and Ontario on the Niagara River — the most powerful waterfall in North America by flow rate, and the classic pairing with an Ontario trip. The Thousand Islands region at the lake\'s eastern outlet, where the St. Lawrence begins: castle-dotted islands (Boldt Castle is the showpiece), best explored by boat tour from Clayton, New York, or Alexandria Bay. On the U.S. south shore: Rochester\'s Seabreeze, the wine trails, and Chimney Bluffs State Park\'s eroded clay formations. Toronto — Canada\'s largest city, worth a trip in itself — anchors the north shore.',
    'Choose Ontario if: you want the falls-plus-city combination, the Thousand Islands, or a trip that pairs naturally with Toronto. Know that: the American shoreline is pleasant but not spectacular; this lake is best as part of a broader upstate New York or cross-border itinerary.',
    'The decision',
    'First trip, summer, classic vacation: Lake Michigan. Beaches, dunes, Traverse City or Door County. Wilderness and drama: Lake Superior. Pictured Rocks, the North Shore, Isle Royale. Quiet water and value: Lake Huron. The Sunrise Side, Alpena\'s shipwrecks. Warm swimming and fishing: Lake Erie. The islands, the walleye charters. Falls and cities: Lake Ontario. Niagara, the Thousand Islands, Toronto.',
    'Whichever you choose, respect the water. The Great Lakes are not big ponds — they produce dangerous rip currents, sudden storms, and waves that have sunk freighters. Check marine forecasts, heed beach warnings, and never underestimate a lake you cannot see across. Treat them like the inland seas they are, and they will give you one of the great American landscapes.',
    ],
