    id: 'seed-savannah-vs-charleston',
    slug: 'savannah-vs-charleston',
    title: 'Savannah vs Charleston: A Weekend Comparison',
    excerpt: 'Savannah or Charleston for a long weekend? An honest comparison of the food, history, walkability, cost, and vibe of the South\'s two great coastal cities.',
    paragraphs: [
    'Savannah and Charleston are the two cities most often mentioned in the same breath in Southern travel, and for good reason. Both are historic coastal cities with cobblestone streets, live oaks draped in Spanish moss, celebrated food scenes, and complicated histories they are still reckoning with. They are about 100 miles apart — an easy two-hour drive — which means travelers constantly face the choice: a long weekend in one, or a rushed taste of both? This is an honest comparison to help you choose.',
    'First impressions',
    'Charleston feels polished. The historic district below Broad Street is meticulously preserved — pastel antebellum mansions, gas lanterns, window boxes overflowing with flowers. It regularly tops "best city" travel lists, and it shows in the prices and the polish. Charleston presents itself as the South at its most refined.',
    'Savannah feels moodier and a little wilder. The city\'s famous grid of 22 park-like squares (24 were originally planned) gives it a rhythm no other American city has — every few blocks, the street opens into a shaded square with a fountain or monument. Savannah is beautiful, but it is also a working port city with a grittier edge, a famous art school (SCAD) injecting youthful energy, and a nightlife that Charleston does not quite match.',
    'Neither is "better." They are different answers to the same question.',
    'History and what to see',
    'Both cities date to the colonial era — Charleston was founded in 1670, Savannah in 1733 — and both were central to the cotton and slave economies of the antebellum South. Any honest visit engages with that history.',
    'In Charleston, start at the Battery and White Point Garden for the harbor views and mansions, then walk the streets south of Broad. The Nathaniel Russell House and the Aiken-Rhett House are the two essential house museums — the latter preserved "as found," which is rarer and more affecting. Fort Sumter, where the Civil War began, is a ferry ride across the harbor. The Old Slave Mart Museum, on the site of a real slave-trading complex, is small and essential. Just across the Cooper River, Boone Hall Plantation is the most visited plantation in the area.',
    'In Savannah, the squares are the attraction — Chippewa (of Forrest Gump bench fame, though the bench itself is in a museum now), Monterey, Madison, each with its own character. Forsyth Park, with its famous fountain, is the city\'s living room. The Owens-Thomas House and the Mercer-Williams House (of Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil fame) anchor the house-museum circuit. Bonaventure Cemetery, made famous by the same book, is genuinely worth the trip — a Victorian cemetery of unusual beauty. The Savannah History Museum and the Ships of the Sea Maritime Museum fill out the picture.',
    'Verdict on sightseeing: Charleston\'s historic core is more concentrated and grander; Savannah\'s is more atmospheric and surprising. History buffs will be satisfied in both.',
    'The food',
    'This is where the rivalry gets serious, and where you, the visitor, win.',
    'Charleston is the more decorated food city — a destination for fine Southern dining. Husk (when it was at its peak), FIG, and Magnolias built the modern reputation; today the scene spans high-end Lowcountry cooking to excellent casual spots. She-crab soup, shrimp and grits, and Frogmore stew are the dishes to seek. The restaurant scene is deep, professional, and expensive.',
    'Savannah punches above its weight with a more eclectic, less formal scene. The Grey — housed in a restored 1938 Greyhound bus terminal, from chef Mashama Bailey — is the flagship, serving refined Southern food with global influences. But Savannah also does low-key brilliantly: Mrs. Wilkes Dining Room (communal tables, fried chicken, a line out the door, cash only at last check), Leopold\'s Ice Cream (since 1919), and a strong showing of soul food, seafood shacks, and SCAD-adjacent creative spots.',
    'Verdict on food: Charleston for the polished destination-dining weekend; Savannah for variety, value, and surprise. You will eat well in both — budget accordingly, because neither is cheap.',
    'Walkability and getting around',
    'Savannah\'s historic district is famously walkable — flat, compact, and gridded around the squares. You can see most of it on foot in a weekend. The free DOT shuttle loops through downtown.',
    'Charleston\'s historic district is also walkable, but it sprawls more, and the best experiences (plantations, beaches, some restaurants) require a car or a tour. The peninsula is walkable; the metro is not.',
    'Verdict: Savannah is the easier car-free weekend. In Charleston, plan on driving or booking tours for at least part of the trip.',
    'Cost',
    'Charleston is the more expensive of the two, by a noticeable margin — hotels in the historic district, especially in spring and fall, are among the priciest in the Southeast. Savannah is not cheap either (Tybee Island and the historic district command real money in season), but your dollar stretches further, particularly for food and lodging.',
    'The beaches',
    'Both cities have easy beach access, and this can tip the decision in summer. Charleston has Sullivan\'s Island, Isle of Palms, and Folly Beach within 20–30 minutes. Savannah has Tybee Island, about 20 minutes east — a classic, slightly scruffy beach town with a lighthouse, a pier, and good shrimp boats. Neither beach rivals Florida\'s, but both are perfectly pleasant summer add-ons.',
    'The honest differences',
    'Nightlife: Savannah wins. River Street\'s open-container district, live music venues, and SCAD-student energy give it a livelier after-dark scene. Charleston is more of a dinner-and-a-cocktail town. Shopping: Charleston wins — King Street is one of the best shopping streets in the South. Savannah\'s Broughton Street and the City Market area are charming but smaller. Day trips: Charleston\'s are stronger (plantations, Angel Oak, Middleton Place). Savannah counters with Tybee Island and easy access to the Golden Isles of Georgia (St. Simons, Jekyll Island) for a longer trip. Crowds: Both are crowded in spring (azalea season) and fall. Charleston\'s peak-season crowds are denser and the city feels more discovered; Savannah absorbs visitors a bit more easily.',
    'So which one?',
    'Choose Charleston if: you want the polished, grand, special-occasion Southern city; fine dining is a priority; you are interested in plantations and the full antebellum-history circuit; you do not mind paying more.',
    'Choose Savannah if: you want atmosphere and walkability; you like a livelier, slightly edgier cultural scene; food variety and value matter; you prefer discovering a city on foot without a car.',
    'Choose both if: you have four or five days. They are two hours apart, and the contrast between them is the point — Charleston\'s polish against Savannah\'s mystery. Drive the coastal route (US-17) between them at least once; the Lowcountry marshes and sea islands are gorgeous.',
    'Either way, go in spring or fall if you can — summer heat and humidity in both cities are serious, and the cities are at their best when you can walk comfortably for hours. Which, in both Savannah and Charleston, is the whole idea.',
    ],
