    id: 'seed-washington-dc-on-a-budget',
    slug: 'washington-dc-on-a-budget',
    title: 'Visiting Washington DC on a Budget',
    excerpt: 'Washington DC is one of America\'s best budget destinations: free Smithsonian museums, free monuments, free tours — plus smart strategies for food, transit, and lodging.',
    paragraphs: [
    'Washington, D.C. is quietly one of the best budget destinations in America. The core visitor experience — the Smithsonian museums, the monuments, the great public buildings — is overwhelmingly free. No other major American city gives away this much. A well-planned D.C. trip can keep daily costs to transit, food, and lodging, with world-class sightseeing costing nothing at all.',
    'The free core: museums',
    'The Smithsonian Institution operates 17 museums and galleries in the D.C. area plus the National Zoo, and admission to all of them is free. Always. This is the foundation of the budget trip. The essential ones:',
    'National Museum of American History — the Star-Spangled Banner (the actual flag), Lincoln\'s top hat, Julia Child\'s kitchen, the lunch counter from the Greensboro sit-in. The single best museum for understanding the country. National Air and Space Museum — the Wright Flyer, Lindbergh\'s Spirit of St. Louis, the Apollo 11 command module. On the Mall; the Udvar-Hazy Center near Dulles Airport (free, but parking costs) holds the Space Shuttle Discovery and an SR-71. National Museum of Natural History — the Hope Diamond, the elephant in the rotunda, the ocean hall, the butterfly pavilion (the pavilion charges a small fee; the rest is free). National Gallery of Art — two buildings spanning Western art from the Middle Ages to the present, plus a sculpture garden with a fountain. The only Leonardo da Vinci painting in the Americas is here. National Museum of African American History and Culture — the newest Mall museum, and the hardest to get into: it requires free timed-entry passes, released in advance online. Book these first, before anything else. National Zoo — free, in the Woodley Park neighborhood. The giant pandas have historically been the draw (check current status — the panda program has had gaps).',
    'Strategy: the museums are enormous. Do not try to "do" more than two in a day — one in the morning, one in the afternoon, with the Mall in between. The American History and Natural History museums each deserve half a day minimum.',
    'The free core: monuments and public spaces',
    'The monuments are open 24 hours and free, and seeing them at night is better than seeing them by day. The Lincoln Memorial, the Washington Monument grounds, the Vietnam Veterans Memorial, the Korean War Veterans Memorial, the World War II Memorial, the Jefferson Memorial across the Tidal Basin — all lit, all nearly empty after 9 p.m., all profoundly more moving in the dark. Walk the Mall end to end at night at least once.',
    'Free tours and entries worth booking:',
    'The U.S. Capitol — free guided tours, booked in advance through the Capitol Visitor Center (or through your representative\'s office, which can sometimes arrange more). The Rotunda and National Statuary Hall are genuinely impressive. The Library of Congress — the Thomas Jefferson Building is the most beautiful public interior in America, and entry is free with a timed pass. The Gutenberg Bible and Jefferson\'s library are on display. The National Archives — the Declaration of Independence, the Constitution, and the Bill of Rights, free, no ticket needed (though lines form — go early or late). The Bureau of Engraving and Printing — free tours showing currency being printed, via timed tickets released online. They go fast. The White House — tours are free but must be requested through your member of Congress, ideally several weeks ahead. Foreign visitors arrange through their embassy. Availability is limited and not guaranteed — request early and have a backup plan. The Kennedy Center\'s Millennium Stage — free performances every evening at 6 p.m., spanning music, dance, and theater. A remarkable free cultural institution hiding in plain sight.',
    'Getting around cheaply',
    'The Metro (subway) is the backbone. Buy a SmarTrip card ($2 for the card, reusable) and load fare; off-peak fares are lower, and the system covers the Mall, the museums, and the neighborhoods. Note that the Mall itself is best done on foot — the distances look short on the map and are longer in person, but walking is the point.',
    'The DC Circulator bus costs $1 per ride and runs useful tourist loops. Regular Metrobus routes take SmarTrip too.',
    'Bikeshare (Capital Bikeshare) is excellent for the Mall and the monuments — the Mall is flat, the bike lanes are good, and a day pass is cheap. Riding along the Tidal Basin at cherry-blossom season is one of the city\'s great experiences.',
    'Avoid rideshares for routine movement; they are the fastest way to burn a D.C. budget. The city is compact and the transit is good.',
    'Eating without the expense-account prices',
    'D.C.\'s reputation for power dining obscures a strong affordable food scene:',
    'Food trucks cluster around the Mall and Farragut Square at lunch — $12–$15 for a serious meal. Union Market and the Eastern Market (Capitol Hill, weekends) offer good cheap eats in lively settings. Ben\'s Chili Bowl on U Street — the half-smoke (D.C.\'s signature sausage) at the historic counter, a civil-rights-era landmark, for under $10. Ethnic corridors are the value play: Eden Center in Falls Church (Vietnamese, a short drive or bus ride into Virginia), the Salvadoran and Ethiopian restaurants along U Street and in Columbia Heights, tacos in Mount Pleasant. Museum cafés are better than they have any right to be — the American Indian Museum\'s Mitsitam Café (Native foods from across the Americas) and the African American Museum\'s Sweet Home Café are legitimate dining destinations, at museum-café prices. Picnic the Mall. Grab sandwiches and eat on the grass between the museums. It is free, the view is the Washington Monument, and it beats a $25 food-court lunch.',
    'Sleeping: the real budget battle',
    'Lodging is the one thing in D.C. that is not cheap. Strategies:',
    'Stay across the river. Arlington and Alexandria, Virginia — especially along the Metro\'s Blue, Yellow, and Orange/Silver lines (Rosslyn, Crystal City, King Street in Alexandria) — are 10–20 minutes from the Mall by train and often significantly cheaper than downtown D.C. Alexandria\'s Old Town is a destination in itself. Hostels exist. D.C. has well-reviewed hostels in central locations at a fraction of hotel prices. Time it right. Avoid cherry-blossom peak (late March to early April — dates shift yearly), major protest/march weekends, and the spring school-trip season (April–May), when hotel prices spike. January, February, and August are the cheapest months — and the museums are climate-controlled. Vacation rentals in residential neighborhoods (Capitol Hill, Petworth, Brookland) can beat hotels for multi-night stays, especially for families.',
    'A sample 3-day budget itinerary',
    'Day 1 — The Mall, east to west. Morning: National Archives (arrive at opening) and the American History Museum. Lunch: food trucks or a Mall picnic. Afternoon: Natural History Museum. Evening: walk the monuments west from the Capitol to the Lincoln Memorial as the lights come on.',
    'Day 2 — The big museums. Morning: Air and Space (or the African American History museum, with your pre-booked timed pass). Afternoon: National Gallery of Art. Evening: free Millennium Stage performance at the Kennedy Center, then dinner on U Street.',
    'Day 3 — Beyond the Mall. Morning: Library of Congress and the Capitol tour (booked ahead). Afternoon: Georgetown (walk the waterfront, window-shop M Street) or the Zoo. Evening: Eastern Market neighborhood for dinner.',
    ],
